/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Absolutism, as a philosophical concept, believes and maintains that moral values are fixed regardless of time, place, and the people concerned. Relativism believes that the concept of right or wrong are not fixed but vary, depending on people, places, circumstances or context. Relativism judges an action in terms of reasons and explanations to determine whether it is wrong or right in that context. Absolutism judges actions by an unbending rule of right and wrong and demands people, places, circumstances and contexts to be tailored to adhere to the absolute rule. The truth is that God is an absolutist and His Word is the absolute. )
/E (Absolutism, as a philosophical concept, believes and maintains that moral values are fixed regardless of time, place, and the people concerned. Relativism believes that the concept of right or wrong are not fixed but vary, depending on people, places, circumstances or context. Relativism judges an action in terms of reasons and explanations to determine whether it is wrong or right in that context. Absolutism judges actions by an unbending rule of right and wrong and demands people, places, circumstances and contexts to be tailored to adhere to the absolute rule. The truth is that God is an absolutist and His Word is the absolute. )
