731 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Up-Line/Vertical Relationship: There are people who have been placed above us in the hierarchical structure of life. Examples are parents, bosses at work, mentors, teachers, pastors, superiors or seniors in a system. We are to accord honour to all people in our relations with them. However, when someone is above us, we owe them particular honour in deference to them. According to Rom. 1: 7, Render therefore to all their dues: tribute to whom tribute is due; custom to whom custom; fear to whom fear; honour to whom honour. There is the generic honour code we must apply in relating with all people. But, there are some specific others that when we honour them, we are giving them their due. 1 Tim 5:17 says, Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine. This shows that elders \(spiritual leaders and elderly people by age\) are deserving of honour. This scripture extends to mean that anyone that is teaching, raising and building you in any regards, \(academic, professionally and most especially in the Word of God\) is deserving of a higher level of honour than you accord people generically. For this reason, teachers and lecturers are deserving of honour from their students and pastors, more especially, are worthy of honour from the people they lead. )
/E (Up-Line/Vertical Relationship: There are people who have been placed above us in the hierarchical structure of life. Examples are parents, bosses at work, mentors, teachers, pastors, superiors or seniors in a system. We are to accord honour to all people in our relations with them. However, when someone is above us, we owe them particular honour in deference to them. According to Rom. 1: 7, Render therefore to all their dues: tribute to whom tribute is due; custom to whom custom; fear to whom fear; honour to whom honour. There is the generic honour code we must apply in relating with all people. But, there are some specific others that when we honour them, we are giving them their due. 1 Tim 5:17 says, Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine. This shows that elders \(spiritual leaders and elderly people by age\) are deserving of honour. This scripture extends to mean that anyone that is teaching, raising and building you in any regards, \(academic, professionally and most especially in the Word of God\) is deserving of a higher level of honour than you accord people generically. For this reason, teachers and lecturers are deserving of honour from their students and pastors, more especially, are worthy of honour from the people they lead. )
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [2]
>>
